== 1. Ideal European partial ladder (nominal N=1) ==
hum         ratio to nominal 0.250  ratio to prime 0.500  cents above hum     0.0
prime       ratio to nominal 0.500  ratio to prime 1.000  cents above hum  1200.0
tierce      ratio to nominal 0.600  ratio to prime 1.200  cents above hum  1515.6
quint       ratio to nominal 0.750  ratio to prime 1.500  cents above hum  1902.0
nominal     ratio to nominal 1.000  ratio to prime 2.000  cents above hum  2400.0
superquint  ratio to nominal 1.500  ratio to prime 3.000  cents above hum  3102.0
strike note (virtual pitch of 2:3:4 series) = N/2 = 0.5 of nominal -> equals prime pitch
tierce/prime = 6/5 (just minor third 315.6 cents; ET minor third 300 cents)
=> a bell sounds 'minor' by design; major-third tuned bells are a special modern design

== 2. Scaling: frequency ~ 1/D, mass ~ D^3 for similar shape ==
calibration D0=1.0 m M0=600.0 kg prime F0=196.0 Hz  (assumption, +/-20% on mass)
1 semitone(s) lower pitch at same shape: mass x1.189, diameter x1.059
12 semitone(s) lower pitch at same shape: mass x8.000, diameter x2.000

Large single bell options (prime Hz -> D, mass)
prime  110.0 Hz (A2) D=1.78 m  mass=   3394 kg
prime  130.8 Hz (C3) D=1.50 m  mass=   2019 kg
prime  165.0 Hz (E3) D=1.19 m  mass=   1006 kg
prime  196.0 Hz (G3) D=1.00 m  mass=    600 kg
prime  261.6 Hz (C4) D=0.75 m  mass=    252 kg
check vs reported Ayodhya bell: 2100 kg ->D= 1.52 m (reported ~5 ft = 1.52 m); prime 129.1 Hz

== 3. Twelve-bell chromatic set, prime tuned ET, A4=440 ==
tabletop C5-B5 total mass 173.4 kg; heaviest 31.5 kg D=0.37 m; lightest 4.7 kg D=0.20 m
hall G3-F#4 total mass 3299.7 kg; heaviest 600.0 kg D=1.00 m; lightest 89.2 kg D=0.53 m

== 4. Tolerance: wall-thickness error -> pitch error ==
D=0.4 m, sound-bow t~27 mm: dt=0.5 mm -> -33..-16 cents; dt=1 mm -> -66..-33 cents; dt=2 mm -> -135..-66 cents; dt=3 mm -> -207..-100 cents
D=1.0 m, sound-bow t~67 mm: dt=0.5 mm -> -13..-7 cents; dt=1 mm -> -26..-13 cents; dt=2 mm -> -53..-26 cents; dt=3 mm -> -80..-39 cents
D=1.5 m, sound-bow t~100 mm: dt=0.5 mm -> -9..-4 cents; dt=1 mm -> -17..-9 cents; dt=2 mm -> -35..-17 cents; dt=3 mm -> -53..-26 cents
1% change in thickness/frequency = 17.2 cents
+/-3 cents = +/-0.17% frequency
+/-5 cents = +/-0.29% frequency
+/-10 cents = +/-0.58% frequency
+/-20 cents = +/-1.16% frequency
+/-50 cents = +/-2.93% frequency
+/-100 cents = +/-5.95% frequency
As-cast scatter if founder holds thickness to +/-3% (sand, core shift): ~ +/-52 cents [I from 1%=17 cents]
Needed to tune to +/-5 cents (0.3%) by turning: remove metal only (lowers some partials, lip cut raises others)

== 5. Metal cost, 78Cu/22Sn ==
base      alloy metal value  22.43 USD/kg = INR    1974/kg (excl. melt loss, GST, fuel, labour)
tin -50%  alloy metal value  16.84 USD/kg = INR    1482/kg (excl. melt loss, GST, fuel, labour)
tin +25%  alloy metal value  25.23 USD/kg = INR    2220/kg (excl. melt loss, GST, fuel, labour)
cast bell     3.0 kg: metal incl 8% melt loss and gating/returns not credited = INR 6,396
cast bell    31.6 kg: metal incl 8% melt loss and gating/returns not credited = INR 67,369
cast bell    60.0 kg: metal incl 8% melt loss and gating/returns not credited = INR 127,915
cast bell   600.0 kg: metal incl 8% melt loss and gating/returns not credited = INR 1,279,151
cast bell  1000.0 kg: metal incl 8% melt loss and gating/returns not credited = INR 2,131,918
cast bell  2100.0 kg: metal incl 8% melt loss and gating/returns not credited = INR 4,477,028
Ayodhya report INR 21 lakh /2100 kg = INR 1000 /kg all-in (vs alloy metal-only INR 1974 /kg) -> not 78/22 tin bronze or price is wrong
Listing asks: INR 2,500/kg (1000 kg brass), 580-750/kg brass church bell, 2,200/kg copper bell-metal church bell

== 6. Doublets: axisymmetry error -> beat ==
split 2 cents at 196 Hz prime = beat 0.23 Hz; at 523 Hz = 0.60 Hz
split 5 cents at 196 Hz prime = beat 0.57 Hz; at 523 Hz = 1.51 Hz
split 10 cents at 196 Hz prime = beat 1.14 Hz; at 523 Hz = 3.03 Hz
split 20 cents at 196 Hz prime = beat 2.28 Hz; at 523 Hz = 6.08 Hz

